#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""nvitop as two boxes of charts, the CPU's above the GPU's, with its tables gone.

Under a clock line, a CPU box, then a GPU box that goes on into nvitop's GPU process list. Each
box is a card: a heading above it (the section's name in bold, then, muted, what changes and has
no chart, then a gray rule to the right edge), rounded corners, faint dotted lines between its
charts so its border is the only strong line, and a time axis at the bottom of its charts. A blank
line sets the GPU's heading apart:
  CPU  load average, and the firmware's power mode (platform_profile: low-power, balanced, ...)
       CPU POWER      yellow  the CPU package's draw, out of its sustained power limit, the lower
                              one if the firmware sets two (109 W here). It can pass it briefly.
       CPU TEMP       red     the CPU package's temperature, out of where it starts slowing itself
                              down to cool (97°C here: 100°C, less the firmware's 3°C offset), from
                              20°C at the bottom
       CPU THROTTLED  blue    the share of the time it slowed itself down: to cool, or because
                              the laptop told it to (a weak charger does); the count doesn't say
       FANS           green   the fastest fan's speed, scaled to what's been seen; the label has
                              each fan's (unlabeled: which is which varies). They cool both chips.
  GPU  its name, its performance state (P0 is the fastest), and its fan if the GPU reports one
       GPU MEM        magenta GPU memory in use, out of the GPU's memory
       GPU UTL        cyan    GPU utilization
       GPU POWER      yellow  out of the power limit (80 W here), which the GPU slows down to stay
                              under; without one, no limit shown and scaled to what's been seen
       GPU TEMP       red     out of where the GPU starts slowing itself down to cool (96°C here),
                              else 100°C, from 20°C at the bottom
       GPU THROTTLED  blue    the share of the time its power limit or its temperature held it
                              back, as NVML counts it
GPU memory and utilization first, in nvitop's order. Then what follows from utilization: load
draws power, power makes heat (temperature lags behind it), and heat makes a chip slow down. With
several GPUs: memory and power summed, utilization and throttling averaged, the hottest
temperature. A chart whose reading the machine doesn't have is left out. The CPU and GPU are
sampled together, so their charts line up column for column.

Throttling: while a chip slows down, a blue band runs down its box, across every chart, darker the
more of the time it slowed; the throttling chart has the amount. Its label has the time
throttled in all since boot, and for the GPU, what held it back: its power limit or heat, each
with its share. (The CPU's counter doesn't say why; nor what the clock was: read after a slowdown,
it's only the clock now.) The kernel counts a CPU slowdown when it ends: they last milliseconds
here, but a long one shows when it's over. A band is on a column exactly where its throttling
chart has a bar: from a quarter of the time on, as less is a chip backing off for milliseconds
after speeding up, which it does all the time. Bands take --colorful (below).

A column is 2 seconds, on the same grid for every chart: the newest fills its left half, then its
right half, then everything moves a column, bands and all. (nvitop's own charts move half a column
every second; a band, a cell's background, can't.)

The tables are gone: what they showed is in the charts and headings, or doesn't change
(versions, bus, ECC: nvitop --once prints them), or is in gnome-system-monitor, beside nvitop on
workspace 8 (CPU use, memory, swap). With several GPUs nvitop's GPU table stays, above the boxes,
for a row per GPU.

The charts take the height there is, leaving room for 3 processes: from 3 rows each up to 8, the
throttling and fan charts half as tall (at least 2). More processes shrink them, and when even
the shortest don't fit, the charts go, leaving the two headings. Each chart's top is its
limit. Its label gives the value, the limit when it isn't 100%, in bold, then muted, the highest
point in view and the rest, like  GPU TEMP: 64°C / 96°C · peak 72°C

CPU power comes from the CPU's energy counter (Intel RAPL, at /sys/class/powercap), which Linux
lets only root read since the PLATYPUS power side channel (2020). initialize.sh adds a udev rule
that lets your group read it. Without it, CPU POWER shows N/A, and it starts once the counter
can be read, without a restart.

Charts glow when they near their limit. Below a chart's top 30% it's a muted shade of its color.
In that 30%, a column turns more vivid the higher it is, up to the most vivid shade of its color
at the top, never white, so maxed-out charts keep their colors apart. The label follows the
latest value, and at the limit becomes a badge: dark text on the chart's color. The glow takes
nvitop's --colorful, on by default here, and a terminal that lets programs set colors, like GNOME
Terminal. Otherwise the charts use the terminal palette's bright colors, and only the badge
shows the limit. NVITOP_MONITOR_MODE=plain nvitop runs without --colorful. Everything else,
arguments included, is plain nvitop, its process list titled GPU Processes.

Runs uv's install of nvitop (uv tool install nvitop) with its screen patched. The patch uses
nvitop internals, written against nvitop 1.6.2: if an upgrade breaks it, ~/.local/bin/nvitop is
the unpatched one.
"""

import curses
import dataclasses
import glob
import itertools
import os
import sys
import threading
import time
import types
from typing import Callable, Optional

# --colorful by default, through nvitop's own setting, read when nvitop is imported. Kept as it is
# if it already says plain or colorful (it does after the re-exec below).
modes = os.environ.get('NVITOP_MONITOR_MODE', '')
if 'plain' not in modes and 'colorful' not in modes:
    os.environ['NVITOP_MONITOR_MODE'] = f'{modes},colorful' if modes else 'colorful'

VENV_PYTHON = os.path.join(
    os.environ.get('XDG_DATA_HOME') or os.path.expanduser('~/.local/share'),
    'uv/tools/nvitop/bin/python',
)

try:
    from nvitop import cli
    from nvitop.api import libnvml
    from nvitop.tui.library import NA, BufferedHistoryGraph
    from nvitop.tui.library.history import HistoryGraph
    from nvitop.tui.library.libcurses import CursesShortcuts
    from nvitop.tui.screens.main import MainScreen
    from nvitop.tui.screens.main.panels import DevicePanel, ProcessPanel
    from nvitop.tui.screens.main.panels.base import BasePanel
    from nvitop.tui.screens.main.panels.host import HostPanel
except ImportError:
    # Started by the system python3 (the shebang). Re-run under the python of uv's nvitop install.
    if sys.executable == VENV_PYTHON:
        raise
    try:
        os.execv(VENV_PYTHON, [VENV_PYTHON, os.path.abspath(__file__), *sys.argv[1:]])
    except FileNotFoundError:
        sys.exit(f'nvitop is not installed (no {VENV_PYTHON}). Run: uv tool install nvitop')

# Rows per chart: the most that fits, between these, with room for PROCESS_ROOM processes.
MIN_CHART_HEIGHT, MAX_CHART_HEIGHT = 3, 8
PROCESS_ROOM = 3
# The charts are braille: 2 samples per column, and a sample is the average over 1 second.
SECONDS_PER_COLUMN = 2
GIB = 2**30

# Palette indices of the bright colors. Each is its normal color + 8, used with only 8 colors.
# --colorful defines its own colors from index 64 up, so it leaves these alone.
RED, GREEN, YELLOW, BLUE, MAGENTA, CYAN = 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14
# Each chart's glow levels, from calm to at its limit: its hue, from muted to as vivid as the
# screen shows it. Designed in OKLCH, so the steps look even. Hues from GNOME Terminal's palette;
# blue's lightness is magenta's, as a lighter blue goes pale, not vivid.
GLOW = {
    MAGENTA: ['#996f9e', '#ae6db6', '#c368ce', '#d862e6', '#ed58ff'],
    CYAN: ['#4a93a0', '#4ea7b7', '#52bcce', '#55d1e7', '#57e7ff'],
    YELLOW: ['#a3915c', '#b9a158', '#d0b251', '#e7c246', '#ffd335'],
    RED: ['#aa6960', '#c0665b', '#d56355', '#ea5d4e', '#ff5545'],
    GREEN: ['#5d966d', '#5aad74', '#56c57c', '#4cdd83', '#39f78a'],
    BLUE: ['#5e82b3', '#528bd5', '#4091f9', '#509bfe', '#62a6ff'],
}
GLOWS_FROM = 0.7  # how far up a chart its glow starts
GLOW_STEPS = 4  # glow levels above calm
READABLE = 2  # labels use at least this level, as the calm color is dim for text
# Throttling bands' backgrounds: GNOME Terminal's background (#171421) tinted with the
# throttling charts' blue, by how much of the time the chip slowed: from each of BAND_STEPS (%).
# Below the first, a CPU only backs off for milliseconds after speeding up. Here, at light load,
# its hottest cores did so in nearly every second, up to 23% of it; with a core busy, 40-54%.
# The throttling charts draw no bar below it, so their bars and the bands agree.
BANDS = ['#1c233b', '#203051', '#253f6a']
BAND_STEPS = (25.0, 50.0, 75.0)
# Held while the snapshot thread adds a sample to every chart, and while a box copies its charts
# to draw them, so each box draws all its charts, and its band, as of the same sample.
SAMPLES = threading.Lock()
# nvitop's double-line box characters, and the rounded single-line ones the boxes use instead, so
# a box reads as one card: its border the only strong line, the lines between charts faint dots.
ROUNDED = str.maketrans('═╒╕╘╛╞╡╤╧╪', '─╭╮╰╯├┤┬┴┼')
# A heading's rule, after its text, to the right edge: the gray of GNOME Terminal's bright black,
# set apart from the boxes' white borders. Tried against a gray rule, a half-height bar or a
# full-height one in the blank line between the boxes: those read as one more line, or crowd the
# GPU's heading, or look like an empty title bar.
RULE_GRAY = '#5e5c64'


def rgb(hex_color):
    """A #rrggbb color in curses' 0-1000 RGB."""
    return tuple(round(int(hex_color[i : i + 2], 16) * 1000 / 255) for i in (1, 3, 5))


def share(percent):
    """A share of the time, like 12%, or <1% for a sliver."""
    return '<1%' if 0 < percent < 0.5 else f'{percent:.0f}%'


def band_level(value):
    """0, or 1 to 3 by how much of the time (a %) the chip throttled."""
    return sum(value >= step for step in BAND_STEPS)


class ChartHistory(BufferedHistoryGraph):
    """nvitop's history of a chart, told when its value was read. take_snapshots gives every chart
    the same time, so they all start a new sample at once: by their own clocks, read a moment
    apart, a second could start between two charts' adds, and put them a column apart."""

    def add_at(self, value, now):
        """nvitop's add, at time now. The buffer holds the readings of the second that started
        at last_update_time. After a gap (a stall), they go in that second, and the seconds with
        no reading are N/A, left blank, where nvitop draws a line from the last value to this
        one. So every chart keeps a sample a second, on the same seconds, of what was read."""
        if value is NA:
            value = self.baseline - 0.1
        elapsed = now - self.last_update_time
        if self.buffer and elapsed >= self.interval:
            HistoryGraph.add(self, sum(self.buffer) / len(self.buffer))
            self.buffer.clear()
            seconds = int(elapsed / self.interval)
            for _ in range(1, seconds):
                HistoryGraph.add(self, self.baseline - 0.1)
            self.last_update_time += seconds * self.interval
        self.buffer.append(value)


class ThrottlingHistory(ChartHistory):
    """A throttling chart's history. Its bars start where the bands do: none below BAND_STEPS[0],
    a dot at least from it. (nvitop draws a dot at 0, and its smallest bars all look alike: on a
    chart 2 rows tall, everything under 15% would look like 0 under a band.)"""

    def make_bar_chart(self, value1, value2):
        def shown(value):
            return value if band_level(value) else self.baseline - 1.0  # below the baseline: blank

        return super().make_bar_chart(shown(value1), shown(value2))


@dataclasses.dataclass
class Chart:
    """One chart: what it shows, its scale and label, its color, and its glow."""

    name: str
    value_of: Callable  # a sample (the GPUs' readings, or the CPU's) -> the value, or NA
    show: Callable  # a value -> its text, like 64 -> '64°C'
    color: int  # palette index of its bright color
    limit: Optional[float]  # the chart's top; None: scaled to what's been seen, no glow
    limit_text: str = ''  # after the value in the label, like ' / 96°C'
    baseline: float = 0.0  # the chart's bottom
    small: bool = False  # half as tall as the others
    now_text: Optional[Callable] = None  # (its latest value) -> the label's value, for show's
    suffix: Optional[Callable] = None  # (its latest value) -> the label's end, after the peak
    throttling: bool = False  # a share of the time throttled: its box's bands follow it

    def __post_init__(self):
        if self.limit is None:
            scale = {'upperbound': 1000.0, 'dynamic_bound': True, 'min_bound': 10.0}
        else:
            scale = {'upperbound': self.limit}
        # Width 77 for now: the panels set the real width and height.
        self.history = (ThrottlingHistory if self.throttling else ChartHistory)(
            width=77, height=self.rows(MIN_CHART_HEIGHT), baseline=self.baseline, interval=1.0,
            **scale,
        )

    def rows(self, chart_height):
        """Its height when the others are chart_height rows."""
        return max(2, chart_height // 2) if self.small else chart_height

    def text(self, value):
        # The history holds values below the baseline where it has none.
        return 'N/A' if value is NA or value < self.baseline else self.show(value)

    def label(self):
        """The label's head, its name and value, and its tail, the peak and the rest."""
        value = self.history.last_value
        known = value is not NA and value >= self.baseline
        now = self.now_text(value) if self.now_text and known else self.text(value)
        suffix = self.suffix(value if known else NA) if self.suffix else ''
        return (f' {self.name}: {now}{self.limit_text} ',
                f'· peak {self.text(self.history.max_value)}{suffix} ')

    def glow(self, value):
        """0 below where the glow starts, up to GLOW_STEPS at the limit."""
        if self.limit is None or value is NA:
            return 0
        start = self.baseline + GLOWS_FROM * (self.limit - self.baseline)
        fraction = (value - start) / (self.limit - start)
        return round(GLOW_STEPS * min(max(fraction, 0.0), 1.0))


class Rate:
    """How fast a counter grows, per second, from its change between two reads.

    Reads closer together than SHORTEST give the last rate again: a counter that ticks every
    millisecond, read twice a few microseconds apart, would look hundreds of times too fast (two
    of nvitop's threads sample at once at the start). A read that fails gives NA, and the next
    one tries again, so a counter that becomes readable starts to show.
    """

    SHORTEST = 0.25  # seconds

    def __init__(self, read, wraps_at=None):
        self.read = read  # () -> the counter, or None if it can't be read
        self.wraps_at = wraps_at
        self.last = None  # (counter, time) at the last read that worked
        self.rate = NA
        self.lock = threading.Lock()

    def __call__(self):
        with self.lock:
            now = time.monotonic()
            if self.last is not None and now - self.last[1] < self.SHORTEST:
                return self.rate
            counter = self.read()
            last, self.last = self.last, None if counter is None else (counter, now)
            self.rate = NA
            if counter is not None and last is not None:
                grown = counter - last[0]
                if grown < 0 and self.wraps_at:  # it wrapped around
                    grown += self.wraps_at
                if grown >= 0:
                    self.rate = grown / (now - last[1])
            return self.rate

    def total(self):
        """The counter at the last read that worked, or None."""
        last = self.last
        return None if last is None else last[0]


def percent_of_time(rate, per_second):
    """A rate of time, counted in units per_second to a second, as a % of the time, at most 100."""
    return NA if rate is NA else min(100.0, 100.0 * rate / per_second)


def duration_text(milliseconds):
    """Like 34m 05s, or 2h 03m, or N/A."""
    if milliseconds is None:
        return 'N/A'
    minutes, seconds = divmod(int(milliseconds) // 1000, 60)
    hours, minutes = divmod(minutes, 60)
    if hours:
        return f'{hours}h {minutes:02d}m'
    return f'{minutes}m {seconds:02d}s' if minutes else f'{seconds}s'


# The CPU's sensors, read from sysfs.

THROTTLED_FILE = '/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/thermal_throttle/package_throttle_total_time_ms'


def read(path, default=None):
    """A file's text, stripped, or default if it can't be read (missing, or root-only)."""
    try:
        with open(path) as file:
            return file.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        return default


def read_int(path):
    try:
        return int(read(path))
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def tcc_offset():
    """How many °C below its critical temperature an Intel CPU starts slowing itself down, as the
    firmware set it (Linux's proc_thermal driver shows it), else 0."""
    for path in glob.glob('/sys/bus/pci/devices/*/tcc_offset_degree_celsius'):
        offset = read_int(path)
        if offset is not None:
            return offset
    return 0


def package_sensor():
    """The CPU package's temperature file, and the temperature it slows down at (else 100°C):
    its critical temperature, less the firmware's offset."""
    for hwmon in sorted(glob.glob('/sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*')):
        if read(f'{hwmon}/name') not in ('coretemp', 'k10temp'):
            continue
        for label in sorted(glob.glob(f'{hwmon}/temp*_label')):
            if read(label) in ('Package id 0', 'Tctl'):
                sensor = label.removesuffix('_label')
                limit = read_int(f'{sensor}_crit') or read_int(f'{sensor}_max')
                return f'{sensor}_input', limit / 1000 - tcc_offset() if limit else 100.0
    return None, 100.0


def fan_files():
    """The fans' speed files, from the first hwmon driver that has any."""
    for hwmon in sorted(glob.glob('/sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*')):
        fans = sorted(glob.glob(f'{hwmon}/fan*_input'))
        if fans:
            return fans
    return []


def energy_rate():
    """The CPU package's energy counter (Intel RAPL, AMD's too) in microjoules, as a Rate."""
    for domain in sorted(glob.glob('/sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl:*')):
        if read(f'{domain}/name') == 'package-0':
            return Rate(lambda: read_int(f'{domain}/energy_uj'),
                        wraps_at=read_int(f'{domain}/max_energy_range_uj'))
    return None


def power_limit():
    """The CPU package's sustained power limit in watts: the lower one if the firmware sets two."""
    limits = []
    for domain in glob.glob('/sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl*:*'):
        if read(f'{domain}/name') != 'package-0':
            continue
        for name in glob.glob(f'{domain}/constraint_*_name'):
            if read(name) == 'long_term':
                microwatts = read_int(name.removesuffix('_name') + '_power_limit_uw')
                if microwatts:
                    limits.append(microwatts / 1e6)
    return min(limits) if limits else None


class Cpu:
    """Where the CPU's readings are, found once at start; sample() reads them."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.temperature_file, self.temperature_limit = package_sensor()
        self.fan_files = fan_files()
        self.energy = energy_rate()
        self.power_limit = power_limit()
        self.throttled = Rate(lambda: read_int(THROTTLED_FILE))
        self.has_throttled = read_int(THROTTLED_FILE) is not None
        self.fans = []  # each fan's speed at the last sample

    def sample(self):
        throttled = percent_of_time(self.throttled(), 1000)  # milliseconds a second
        milli_celsius = read_int(self.temperature_file) if self.temperature_file else None
        self.fans = [speed for speed in map(read_int, self.fan_files) if speed is not None]
        microwatts = self.energy() if self.energy else NA
        return types.SimpleNamespace(
            watts=NA if microwatts is NA else microwatts / 1e6,
            temperature=NA if milli_celsius is None else milli_celsius / 1000,
            throttled=throttled,
            fastest_fan=max(self.fans) if self.fans else NA,
            load_average=os.getloadavg(),
            profile=read('/sys/firmware/acpi/platform_profile'),
        )


CPU = Cpu()


def throttled_since_boot(milliseconds):
    """The label's end: the time throttled in all since boot, like  · 46m 03s throttled since boot"""
    return f' · {duration_text(milliseconds)} throttled since boot'


def make_cpu_charts():
    """The CPU's charts, leaving out those it has no reading for."""
    charts = []
    if CPU.energy:
        limit = CPU.power_limit
        charts.append(Chart('CPU POWER', lambda sample: sample.watts, '{:.1f} W'.format, YELLOW,
                            limit=limit, limit_text='' if limit is None else f' / {limit:.0f} W'))
    if CPU.temperature_file:
        charts.append(Chart('CPU TEMP', lambda sample: sample.temperature, '{:.0f}°C'.format, RED,
                            limit=CPU.temperature_limit, baseline=20.0,
                            limit_text=f' / {CPU.temperature_limit:.0f}°C'))
    if CPU.has_throttled:
        charts.append(Chart(
            'CPU THROTTLED', lambda sample: sample.throttled, share, BLUE, limit=100.0, small=True,
            now_text=lambda value: f'{share(value)} of the time',
            suffix=lambda value: throttled_since_boot(CPU.throttled.total()), throttling=True,
        ))
    if CPU.fan_files:
        charts.append(Chart(
            'FANS', lambda sample: sample.fastest_fan, '{:.0f} RPM'.format, GREEN, limit=None,
            small=True, now_text=lambda value: ' · '.join(map(str, CPU.fans)) + ' RPM',
        ))
    return charts


# The GPUs' readings, from NVML.


def nvml_ints(devices, query, *args):
    """The NVML query's value for each device, or None if any device doesn't report one."""
    values = [libnvml.nvmlQuery(query, device.handle, *args) for device in devices]
    return values if values and all(isinstance(value, int) for value in values) else None


def held_back_ns(devices, policy):
    """Nanoseconds the GPUs' power limit or temperature (policy) has held them back, or None."""
    total = 0
    for device in devices:
        status = libnvml.nvmlQuery('nvmlDeviceGetViolationStatus', device.handle, policy)
        if not hasattr(status, 'violationTime'):  # NA: not supported
            return None
        total += status.violationTime
    return total


class GpuThrottling:
    """How much of the time the GPUs' power limit and temperature hold them back."""

    def __init__(self, devices):
        self.devices = devices
        self.power = Rate(lambda: held_back_ns(devices, libnvml.NVML_PERF_POLICY_POWER))
        self.heat = Rate(lambda: held_back_ns(devices, libnvml.NVML_PERF_POLICY_THERMAL))
        self.shares = (NA, NA)  # power's and heat's, at the last sample

    def supported(self):
        return bool(self.devices) and None not in (
            held_back_ns(self.devices, libnvml.NVML_PERF_POLICY_POWER),
            held_back_ns(self.devices, libnvml.NVML_PERF_POLICY_THERMAL),
        )

    def sample(self, readings):
        per_second = 1e9 * len(self.devices)  # nanoseconds a second, for each GPU
        self.shares = power, heat = (percent_of_time(self.power(), per_second),
                                     percent_of_time(self.heat(), per_second))
        if NA in self.shares:
            return NA
        return min(100.0, power + heat)

    def now_text(self, value):
        names = ('power limit', 'heat')
        reasons = [f'{name} {share(part)}' for name, part in zip(names, self.shares)
                   if part is not NA and part > 0]
        return f'{share(value)} of the time' + (f' ({", ".join(reasons)})' if reasons else '')

    def suffix(self, value):
        totals = [self.power.total(), self.heat.total()]
        return throttled_since_boot(None if None in totals else sum(totals) / 1e6)  # milliseconds


def make_gpu_charts(devices):
    def known(readings, field):
        return [getattr(s, field) for s in readings if getattr(s, field) is not NA]

    def memory_gib(readings):
        used = known(readings, 'memory_used')
        return sum(used) / GIB if used else NA

    def memory_percent(readings):
        used, total = known(readings, 'memory_used'), known(readings, 'memory_total')
        return 100.0 * sum(used) / sum(total) if used and total and sum(total) > 0 else NA

    def utilization(readings):
        values = known(readings, 'gpu_utilization')
        return sum(values) / len(values) if values else NA

    def power(readings):
        values = known(readings, 'power_usage')  # milliwatts
        return sum(values) / 1000.0 if values else NA

    def temperature(readings):
        values = known(readings, 'temperature')
        return max(values) if values else NA

    totals = [device.memory_total() for device in devices]
    if totals and all(isinstance(total, int) for total in totals):
        total = sum(totals) / GIB
        memory = Chart('GPU MEM', memory_gib, '{:.1f} GiB'.format, MAGENTA, limit=total,
                       limit_text=f' / {total:.0f} GiB')
    else:
        memory = Chart('GPU MEM', memory_percent, '{:.1f}%'.format, MAGENTA, limit=100.0)

    # nvitop's own power limit query comes back N/A on this laptop GPU; the enforced limit doesn't.
    limits = nvml_ints(devices, 'nvmlDeviceGetEnforcedPowerLimit')
    power_limit = sum(limits) / 1000.0 if limits else None
    slowdowns = nvml_ints(
        devices,
        'nvmlDeviceGetTemperatureThreshold',
        libnvml.NVML_TEMPERATURE_THRESHOLD_SLOWDOWN,
    )
    slowdown = float(min(slowdowns)) if slowdowns else 100.0

    charts = [
        memory,
        Chart('GPU UTL', utilization, '{:.1f}%'.format, CYAN, limit=100.0),
        Chart('GPU POWER', power, '{:.1f} W'.format, YELLOW, limit=power_limit, limit_text=(
            '' if power_limit is None else f' / {power_limit:.0f} W'
        )),
        Chart('GPU TEMP', temperature, '{:.0f}°C'.format, RED, limit=slowdown, baseline=20.0,
              limit_text=f' / {slowdown:.0f}°C'),
    ]
    throttling = GpuThrottling(devices)
    if throttling.supported():
        charts.append(Chart('GPU THROTTLED', throttling.sample, share, BLUE, limit=100.0,
                            small=True, now_text=throttling.now_text, suffix=throttling.suffix,
                            throttling=True))
    return charts


def gpu_readings(devices):
    """What the GPU charts show, for each GPU, read now, at the same time as the CPU and the GPUs'
    throttling. (The devices' snapshots, nvitop's GPU table's, come from a thread of its own, at
    most once a second, up to 1.5 s old: bands ran ahead of the GPU charts.)"""
    readings = []
    for device in devices:
        with device.oneshot():
            readings.append(types.SimpleNamespace(
                memory_used=device.memory_used(), memory_total=device.memory_total(),
                gpu_utilization=device.gpu_utilization(), power_usage=device.power_usage(),
                temperature=device.temperature(),
            ))
    return readings


def throttling_chart(charts):
    return next((chart for chart in charts if chart.throttling), None)


# Drawing the boxes.


def frame_width(panel):
    """The boxes' width: the terminal's, at least 79, as the process list's under them. (nvitop
    draws its GPU table 79 wide below 100 columns.)"""
    return panel.width


def box_rows(charts, chart_height):
    """The rows charts take, each with the line under it, when the tall ones are chart_height."""
    return sum(chart.rows(chart_height) + 1 for chart in charts)


def box_top(width):
    return '╭' + '─' * (width - 2) + '╮'


def chart_frame(width, charts, bottom):
    """The sides of the charts, at their heights, a row under each (draw_charts dots the ones
    between them), the last the bottom border, with corners bottom."""
    data_line = '│' + ' ' * (width - 2) + '│'
    lines = []
    for chart in charts:
        lines += [data_line] * (chart.history.height + 1)
    lines[-1] = bottom[0] + '─' * (width - 2) + bottom[1]
    return lines


def draw_heading(panel, y, parts):
    """A box's heading, above it, like  CPU  load 0.53 0.87 0.96 · power mode balanced  ━━━━━━.

    parts: the section's name, in bold, then what follows it, muted, then a gray rule to the right
    edge. Parts that don't fit are left out, from the end.
    """
    width = frame_width(panel)
    name, rest = parts[0], list(parts[1:])
    while rest and len(f' {name}  ' + ' · '.join(rest)) > width:
        rest.pop()
    panel.addstr(y, panel.x, ' ' * width)
    panel.addstr(y, panel.x + 1, name, panel.get_fg_bg_attr(attr='bold'))
    panel.addstr(y, panel.x + len(name) + 3, ' · '.join(rest), panel.get_fg_bg_attr(attr='dim'))
    end = len(f' {name}  ' + ' · '.join(rest)) + 2
    if end < width:
        true_color = panel.TERM_256COLOR and curses.can_change_color()
        rule = panel.get_fg_bg_attr(fg=rgb(RULE_GRAY)) if true_color else panel.get_fg_bg_attr(
            attr='dim')
        panel.addstr(y, panel.x + end, '━' * (width - end), rule)


def draw_time_marks(panel, bottom):
    """Time marks along a bottom border: 30s, 60s, then every minute, newest on the right."""
    newest_column = panel.x + frame_width(panel) - 2
    for seconds in itertools.chain((30, 60), itertools.count(120, 60)):
        mark = f' {seconds}s┴'
        start = newest_column - seconds // SECONDS_PER_COLUMN - len(mark) + 1
        if start <= panel.x:
            break
        panel.addstr(bottom, start, mark)
        panel.color_at(bottom, start + 1, width=len(mark) - 2, attr='dim')


def column_samples(history, width):
    """A chart's columns, left to right, each its (older, newer) sample, None for one not in yet.

    A column holds 2 seconds on a fixed grid, the same for every chart, counted from their shared
    start: the newest fills its older half, then its newer half, then a new column starts. So the
    charts and their box's band move a whole column at once, every 2 seconds. (nvitop's own graph
    moves half a column every second, and a band, a cell's background, can't: it lagged behind
    every other second.)
    """
    samples = round((history.last_update_time - history.start_time) / history.interval)
    values = list(history.reversed_history)  # newest first
    if samples % 2 == 0:  # the newest column's newer half isn't in yet
        values.insert(0, None)
    width = min(width, (len(values) - 1) // 2)
    return [(values[2 * k + 1], values[2 * k]) for k in reversed(range(width))]


def known_max(pair):
    return max((value for value in pair if value is not None), default=0)


def bar_lines(history, columns):
    """A chart's rows of braille, top to bottom, from its columns."""
    blank = history.baseline - 1.0  # below the baseline: no dots
    bars = [history.make_bar_chart(blank if older is None else older,
                                   blank if newer is None else newer)
            for older, newer in columns]
    return [''.join(row) for row in zip(*bars)] if bars else []


def copy_charts(charts):
    """Each chart's columns, its rows of braille, its label and the label's glow, all as of the
    same sample. The rows are made again only when a sample comes in or the chart's size or scale
    changes."""
    copies = []
    with SAMPLES:
        for chart in charts:
            history = chart.history
            with history.write_lock:
                key = (history.last_update_time, history.width, history.height, history.bound)
                if getattr(chart, 'drawn', (None,))[0] != key:
                    columns = column_samples(history, history.width)
                    chart.drawn = (key, columns, bar_lines(history, columns))
                _, columns, lines = chart.drawn
                copies.append(types.SimpleNamespace(
                    columns=columns, lines=lines, height=history.height, label=chart.label(),
                    glow=chart.glow(history.last_value),
                ))
    return copies


def draw_charts(panel, charts, top):
    """Each chart's line and label, the first at row top, each under the one before it, and the
    throttling bands down them, but for their labels, and across the separators between them."""
    # Glowing and bands take colors of our own: --colorful, and a terminal that lets curses set
    # colors.
    true_color = panel.TERM_256COLOR and curses.can_change_color()
    bright = getattr(curses, 'COLORS', 0) >= 16
    copies = copy_charts(charts)
    band_chart = throttling_chart(charts) if true_color else None
    # Each column's band: 0, or 1 to 3 by how much of the time the chip throttled.
    bands = [0] * (frame_width(panel) - 2)
    if band_chart:
        bands = [band_level(known_max(pair)) for pair in copies[charts.index(band_chart)].columns]

    def background(band):
        return rgb(BANDS[band - 1]) if band else -1

    def banded(row, column, text, attr_of, bands):
        """text from column on, each run of columns on its band's background."""
        def band_at(j):
            return bands[j] if j < len(bands) else 0

        for band, run in itertools.groupby(range(column, column + len(text)), key=band_at):
            run = list(run)
            panel.addstr(row, panel.x + 1 + run[0], text[run[0] - column : run[-1] - column + 1],
                         attr_of(band))

    chart_top = top
    for index, (chart, copy) in enumerate(zip(charts, copies)):
        attrs = {}

        def attr(level, band=0, label=None):
            """Bars glow by level. A label's head is bold, and at the limit, a badge: dark on its
            color. Its tail is muted."""
            if (level, band, label) not in attrs:
                if true_color:
                    shade = {'head': max(level, READABLE), 'tail': 1}.get(label, level)
                    fg = rgb(GLOW[chart.color][shade])
                else:  # flat colors; at the limit only the badge shows it
                    fg = chart.color if bright else chart.color - 8
                style = {'head': 'bold | reverse' if level == GLOW_STEPS else 'bold',
                         'tail': 0 if true_color else 'dim'}.get(label, 0)
                attrs[level, band, label] = panel.get_fg_bg_attr(
                    fg=fg, bg=background(band), attr=style
                )
            return attrs[level, band, label]

        lines, width = copy.lines, len(copy.columns)
        levels = [chart.glow(known_max(pair)) for pair in copy.columns]  # glows by the higher
        chart_bands = (bands + [0] * width)[:width]
        runs = [(key, len(list(run))) for key, run in itertools.groupby(zip(levels, chart_bands))]

        for row, line in enumerate(lines):
            column = 0
            for (level, band), length in runs:
                text = line[column : column + length]
                panel.addstr(chart_top + row, panel.x + 1 + column, text, attr(level, band))
                column += length
        head, tail = copy.label
        room = frame_width(panel) - 2
        # Labels stay on the plain background: on a band's, its edge ran through the text.
        panel.addstr(chart_top, panel.x + 1, head[:room], attr(copy.glow, label='head'))
        if len(head) < room:
            panel.addstr(chart_top, panel.x + 1 + len(head), tail[: room - len(head)],
                         attr(0, label='tail'))
        chart_top += copy.height

        if index < len(charts) - 1:  # the faint line under it, banded too
            banded(chart_top, 0, '┈' * width,
                   lambda band: panel.get_fg_bg_attr(bg=background(band), attr='dim'), bands)
        chart_top += 1


def gpu_title(host_panel):
    """The GPU box's title: the GPU, its performance state, and its fan if it reports one."""
    if len(host_panel.devices) != 1:
        return ['GPU', f'{len(host_panel.devices)} GPUs']
    snapshot = host_panel.devices[0].snapshot
    parts = ['GPU', host_panel.gpu_name, f'performance state {snapshot.performance_state}']
    if 'N/A' not in str(snapshot.fan_speed_string):
        parts.append(f'fan {snapshot.fan_speed_string}')
    return parts


# The GPU box: nvitop's host panel, its CPU, RAM and swap charts replaced: its heading, the box's
# top, the charts. It also samples the CPU, in the same thread, so the CPU and GPU charts line up.
# Its bottom line goes on into the process list's frame below. Without charts, it's the heading and
# the top of the process list's frame.


def set_width(self, value):
    width = max(79, value)
    if self._width != width:
        if self.visible:
            self.need_redraw = True
        # The original __init__ sets the width before init() below has made the charts.
        for chart in getattr(self, 'charts', ()):
            chart.history.width = width - 2
    self._width = width


def take_snapshots(self):
    """Reads everything, then adds it to every chart at once, at one time. (Added as each was read,
    between slow reads, NVML's and the fans' 20 ms, a second could start between two charts.)"""
    readings = gpu_readings(self.devices)
    values = [chart.value_of(readings) for chart in self.charts]
    self.cpu_sample = sample = CPU.sample()
    values += [chart.value_of(sample) for chart in self.cpu_charts]
    now = time.monotonic()
    with SAMPLES:
        for chart, value in zip(self.charts + self.cpu_charts, values):
            chart.history.add_at(value, now)


def frame_lines(self, compact=None):
    if compact is None:
        compact = self.compact
    if compact or self.no_unicode:
        return []
    width = frame_width(self)
    return [box_top(width), *chart_frame(width, self.charts, '├┤')]  # under the heading


def draw(self):
    self.color_reset()
    boxed = not self.compact
    if self.need_redraw:
        for y, line in enumerate(self.frame_lines() or [box_top(frame_width(self))],
                                 start=self.y + 1):
            self.addstr(y, self.x, line)
        if boxed:
            draw_time_marks(self, self.y + self.height - 1)
    draw_heading(self, self.y, gpu_title(self))
    if boxed:
        draw_charts(self, self.charts, self.y + 2)
    self.color_reset()


original_init = HostPanel.__init__


def init(self, *args, **kwargs):
    original_init(self, *args, **kwargs)
    self.charts = make_gpu_charts(self.devices)
    for chart in self.charts:
        chart.history.width = frame_width(self) - 2
    self.gpu_name = str(self.devices[0].name()).removeprefix('NVIDIA ') if self.devices else ''
    self.cpu_charts = make_cpu_charts()  # drawn by the CPU panel
    # One clock for every chart's seconds, so a band's columns are every chart's.
    start = time.monotonic()
    for chart in self.charts + self.cpu_charts:
        chart.history.start_time = chart.history.last_update_time = start
    self.cpu_sample = CPU.sample()
    self.full_height = 2 + box_rows(self.charts, MIN_CHART_HEIGHT)  # and the heading and top
    self.compact_height = 2  # the heading, and the top of the process list's frame
    self.height = self.compact_height if self.compact else self.full_height


HostPanel.__init__ = init
HostPanel.width = property(HostPanel.width.fget, set_width)
HostPanel.take_snapshots = take_snapshots
HostPanel.frame_lines = frame_lines
HostPanel.draw = draw
HostPanel.print = lambda self: None  # nvitop --once: no CPU and RAM bars


class CpuPanel(BasePanel):
    """The clock line, the CPU box's heading, the box, and a blank line under it.

    Without charts, there's the heading only, and no blank line.
    """

    NAME = 'cpu'

    def __init__(self, host_panel, device_panel, *, win, root):
        super().__init__(win, root)
        self.host_panel = host_panel  # it samples the CPU
        self.device_panel = device_panel  # when it shows, it has the clock line
        self.charts = host_panel.cpu_charts
        self.no_unicode = False
        self._show_charts = True
        self.chart_height = MIN_CHART_HEIGHT
        self.width = max(79, root.width)
        self.height = self.height_for(True, self.chart_height)

    @property
    def width(self):
        return self._width

    @width.setter
    def width(self, value):
        width = max(79, value)
        if self._width != width:
            if self.visible:
                self.need_redraw = True
            for chart in self.charts:
                chart.history.width = width - 2
        self._width = width

    def boxed(self, show_charts=None):
        show_charts = self._show_charts if show_charts is None else show_charts
        return show_charts and bool(self.charts) and not self.no_unicode

    def height_for(self, show_charts, chart_height):
        if not self.boxed(show_charts):
            return 2  # the clock line and the heading
        # and the box's top, the charts, and a blank line
        return 3 + box_rows(self.charts, chart_height) + 1

    @property
    def show_charts(self):
        return self.boxed()

    @show_charts.setter
    def show_charts(self, value):
        if self._show_charts != value:
            self._show_charts = value
            self.need_redraw = True
        self.height = self.height_for(value, self.chart_height)

    def draw(self):
        self.color_reset()
        width = frame_width(self)
        boxed = self.boxed()

        if self.need_redraw:
            for y in (self.y, self.y + self.height - 1):  # the clock line, the blank line
                self.addstr(y, self.x, ' ' * width)
            if boxed:
                lines = [box_top(width), *chart_frame(width, self.charts, '╰╯')]
                for y, line in enumerate(lines, start=self.y + 2):
                    self.addstr(y, self.x, line)
                draw_time_marks(self, self.y + self.height - 2)
        self.draw_clock_line(width)

        sample = self.host_panel.cpu_sample
        load_average = ' '.join(f'{value:.2f}' for value in sample.load_average)
        parts = ['CPU', f'load {load_average}']
        if sample.profile:
            parts.append(f'power mode {sample.profile}')
        draw_heading(self, self.y + 1, parts)
        if boxed:
            draw_charts(self, self.charts, self.y + 3)
        self.color_reset()

    def draw_clock_line(self, width):
        """nvitop's clock and help hint, or its hint for signalling a selected process.

        With nvitop's GPU table above, which has the clock and help hint, the line is blank but for
        the signal hint.
        """
        signalling = getattr(self.parent.process_panel, 'signal_hint', False)
        if signalling:
            hint = SIGNAL_HINT
            keys = [(7, 2, 'magenta'), (10, 3, 'red'), (15, 1, 'magenta'), (17, 4, 'red'),
                    (23, 1, 'magenta'), (25, 4, 'red')]
        else:
            hint = '(Press h for help or q to quit)'
            keys = [(7, 1, 'magenta'), (21, 1, 'magenta')]
        clock = time.strftime('%a %b %d %H:%M:%S %Y')
        if self.device_panel.height:
            clock = ''
            if not signalling:
                self.addstr(self.y, self.x, ' ' * width)
                return
        start = max(width - len(hint), len(clock) + 2)
        self.addstr(self.y, self.x, clock.ljust(start) + hint)
        for offset, length, color in keys:
            attr = 'bold | italic' if color == 'magenta' else 'bold'
            self.color_at(self.y, self.x + start + offset, width=length, fg=color, attr=attr)

    def print_width(self):
        return self.width if self.width >= 100 else 79

    def print(self):  # nvitop --once: nvitop's own output only
        pass


# nvitop's GPU table: with one GPU, it takes no lines, and the CPU panel has the clock line.

original_device_init = DevicePanel.__init__
original_device_draw = DevicePanel.draw


def shows_gpu_table(panel):
    return panel.device_count != 1 or panel.mig_device_count > 0


def device_init(self, *args, **kwargs):
    original_device_init(self, *args, **kwargs)
    if not shows_gpu_table(self):
        self.full_height = self.compact_height = self.height = 0


def device_draw(self):
    if self.height:
        original_device_draw(self)


DevicePanel.__init__ = device_init
DevicePanel.draw = device_draw

# nvitop's process list, titled GPU Processes. On screen, it goes on from the GPU box: the box's
# bottom line is its top border (its own is left out), the box's last chart row is its first line,
# and its frame is rounded like the boxes. nvitop writes its hint for signalling a selected process
# on that first line (and blanks it otherwise): that goes to the clock line instead.
original_process_header_lines = ProcessPanel.header_lines
original_process_draw = ProcessPanel.draw
SIGNAL_HINT = '(Press ^C(INT)/T(TERM)/K(KILL) to send signals)'


def process_header_lines(self):
    lines = original_process_header_lines(self)
    lines[1] = lines[1].replace('Processes:    ', 'GPU Processes:', 1)
    if getattr(self, 'in_gpu_box', False):
        lines[0] = ''
    return lines


def process_draw(self):
    hint_row, written = self.y, []

    def addstr(*args, **kwargs):
        if args and args[0] == hint_row:
            written.append(str(args[2]) if len(args) > 2 else '')
            return
        if len(args) > 2 and isinstance(args[2], str):  # its frame, rounded like the boxes
            args = (*args[:2], args[2].translate(ROUNDED), *args[3:])
        CursesShortcuts.addstr(self, *args, **kwargs)

    def color_at(y, *args, **kwargs):
        if y != hint_row:
            CursesShortcuts.color_at(self, y, *args, **kwargs)

    self.in_gpu_box = True  # drawing on screen, not printing for nvitop --once
    self.addstr, self.color_at = addstr, color_at
    try:
        original_process_draw(self)
    finally:
        del self.addstr, self.color_at
        self.in_gpu_box = False
    self.signal_hint = SIGNAL_HINT in written


ProcessPanel.header_lines = process_header_lines
ProcessPanel.draw = process_draw
JOINED_ROWS = 2  # the GPU box's and the process list's rows that are the same


# nvitop's main screen: the GPU table (with several GPUs), the CPU box (the CPU panel), the GPU box
# (the host panel), and the processes in it.


def panels(self):
    return self.device_panel, self.cpu_panel, self.host_panel, self.process_panel


def stack_panels(self):
    """Each panel under the one before it, the processes in the GPU box. Returns their height."""
    y = self.y
    for panel in panels(self):
        panel.y = y
        y += panel.height
    self.process_panel.y -= JOINED_ROWS
    return y - JOINED_ROWS - self.y


original_main_init = MainScreen.__init__


def main_init(self, *args, **kwargs):
    original_main_init(self, *args, **kwargs)
    self.cpu_panel = CpuPanel(self.host_panel, self.device_panel, win=self.win, root=self.root)
    self.cpu_panel.focused = False
    self.cpu_panel.no_unicode = self.no_unicode
    self.cpu_panel.x = self.x
    self.add_child(self.cpu_panel)
    self.height = stack_panels(self)


def set_chart_height(self, chart_height):
    cpu, host = self.cpu_panel, self.host_panel
    for chart in cpu.charts + host.charts:
        if chart.history.height != chart.rows(chart_height):
            chart.history.height = chart.rows(chart_height)
            cpu.need_redraw = host.need_redraw = True
    cpu.chart_height = chart_height
    if not self.no_unicode:
        host.full_height = 2 + box_rows(host.charts, chart_height)
        host.height = host.compact_height if host.compact else host.full_height


def update_size(self, termsize=None):
    """nvitop's, with the CPU panel, and the charts as tall as fits."""
    n_term_lines, n_term_cols = termsize = super(MainScreen, self).update_size(termsize=termsize)

    self.width = n_term_cols - self.x
    for panel in panels(self):
        panel.width = self.width

    self.y = min(self.y, self.root.y)
    height = n_term_lines - self.y
    device, cpu, host, process = panels(self)

    def boxes(chart_height):  # the CPU box, and the GPU box without the processes
        return cpu.height_for(True, chart_height) + 2 + box_rows(host.charts, chart_height)

    # The tallest charts that leave room for PROCESS_ROOM processes (the list's frame, and a blank
    # line above it, take 6 lines), at least the shortest.
    room = max(process.full_height, 6 + PROCESS_ROOM) - JOINED_ROWS
    chart_height = next(
        (h for h in range(MAX_CHART_HEIGHT, MIN_CHART_HEIGHT, -1)
         if device.full_height + boxes(h) + room <= height),
        MIN_CHART_HEIGHT,
    )
    set_chart_height(self, chart_height)

    processes = process.full_height - JOINED_ROWS
    heights = [
        device.full_height + boxes(chart_height) + processes,
        device.compact_height + boxes(chart_height) + processes,
        device.compact_height + cpu.height_for(False, chart_height) + host.compact_height
        + processes,
    ]
    if self.mode == 'auto':
        self.compact = height < heights[0]
        host.compact = height < heights[1]
        process.compact = height < heights[-1]
    else:
        self.compact = self.mode == 'compact'
        host.compact = self.compact
        process.compact = self.compact
    device.compact = self.compact
    cpu.show_charts = not host.compact

    height = stack_panels(self)

    if self.y < self.root.y and self.y + height < n_term_lines:
        self.y = min(self.root.y + self.root.height - height, self.root.y)
        self.update_size(termsize)
        self.need_redraw = True

    if self.height != height:
        self.height = height
        self.need_redraw = True

    return termsize


def main_poke(self):
    super(MainScreen, self).poke()

    height = sum(panel.height for panel in panels(self)) - JOINED_ROWS
    if self.height != height:
        self.update_size()
        self.need_redraw = True


MainScreen.__init__ = main_init
MainScreen.update_size = update_size
MainScreen.poke = main_poke

if __name__ == '__main__':
    sys.exit(cli.main())
